    "result_text": "Done. The build passes and the daily-limit acceptance test (`coreRejectsOverDailyLimit`) is green.\n\n## What I did\n\nThe repo already had a test asserting that the 101st owner created today gets a 429. I implemented the behaviour by mirroring the existing `EnsureCityHasCapacity` pattern exactly, keeping every changed unit small and self-contained:\n\n**New files (each a small cohesive unit, no surrounding class complexity):**\n- `EnsureDailyRegistrationCapacity.java` \u2014 one `service` method that counts existing owners whose `registrationDate` equals the new owner's (today, set by `BuildOwner`), excluding self by id, and throws when the count reaches 100.\n- `DailyRegistrationLimitException.java` \u2014 checked exception (`extends Exception`).\n- `DailyRegistrationLimitExceptionHandler.java` \u2014 responds `429 TOO_MANY_REQUESTS` via the shared `ProblemDetails` builder.\n- Escalation YAML mapping the exception to its handler.\n\n**One edited file:**\n- `owners.POST.yml` \u2014 inserted an `ensureDailyCapacity` step between `ensureCityCapacity` and `assignCustomerCode`, under the same `transaction` governance.\n\n## Why this keeps structural impact low\nI added behaviour as new small classes (WMC_other = 0 \u2192 the `max(WMC_other,1)` term is 1, and each new method has CC \u2248 2) rather than growing any existing method. The only pre-existing code touched is the YAML pipeline wiring \u2014 not a function with cyclomatic complexity \u2014 so no large class pays for the change.",
